    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=26d7eba1-7ff1-4642-a01e-31acc49de6f5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201786366829_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Simulation Technician",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\n\nLocation: Gwynedd Valley\n\nThe Frances M. Maguire College of Nursing and Health Professions at Gwynedd Mercy University is seeking a dynamic and skilled professional to join our simulation program. This is a full-time, 12-month staff position. Our new 63,000-square-foot Frances M. Maguire Healthcare Innovation Center is the centerpiece of the Frances M. Maguire Healthcare Innovation Campus. This state-of-the-art facility features immersive simulation and skills labs where students practice clinical skills and manage complex patient scenarios in a safe, realistic environment. The Center also fosters interprofessional collaboration across nursing, allied health, and other healthcare programs, preparing graduates to excel in today’s team-based care settings. Overview of the Position : The Simulation Technician manages the daily operations, scheduling, and technical oversight of the simulation laboratories within the Frances M. Maguire College of Nursing and Health Professions. This position applies advanced technical, instructional, and compliance expertise to support Nursing and Health professions education through high-fidelity simulation, virtual simulation, and related technologies. Working in close collaboration with simulation coordinator/associates and faculty, the Simulation Technician implements and evaluates simulation activities that align with curriculum objectives and accreditation standards. The role requires independent judgment, strategic planning, and accountability for ensuring simulation operations meet institutional goals, safety requirements, and quality benchmarks. The Simulation Technician reports to the Dean and works closely with the Simulation Coordinator/Associates and Simulation Laboratory faculty. Essential Duties & Responsibilities  include the following. Other duties may be assigned.  1. Simulation Operations Management Coordinate simulation activities, including scheduling, preparing and setting up equipment, executing technical operations during events, and tearing down afterward, while ensuring proper care, maintenance, and readiness of all equipment. Operate, program, and troubleshoot high-fidelity simulators, task trainers, virtual simulation platforms, and audiovisual systems. Develop and implement simulation lab procedures, ensuring safety, efficiency, and compliance with best practices. Maintain and oversee simulation equipment, coordinating purchases, repairs, and vendor relationships for service and upgrades, while also managing a complete inventory of all equipment, supplies, materials, and other technologies used in the laboratory. 2. Curriculum Integration & Faculty Partnership Collaborate with Simulation Coordinator/Associates and faculty to implement simulation-based learning experiences and respond to changing needs in simulation practices and operation that align with current curricular needs and accreditation standards. Provide real-time technical and operational support during simulations, ensuring smooth delivery of instructional activities. Advise faculty on technology integration and simulation pedagogy, providing training as needed.  3. Compliance, Safety & Quality Assurance Ensure simulation operations comply with accreditation standards (e.g., INACSL) and institutional policies. Maintain accurate records of simulation sessions, usage metrics, equipment maintenance, and compliance documentation. Implement quality improvement processes based on feedback and performance data.  4. Training & Capacity Building Develop and deliver training programs for faculty, staff, and student assistants on simulation technology, equipment care, and scenario operations. Serve as the primary resource for technical expertise within the College. Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree in healthcare, biomedical engineering, instructional technology, or related field (Master’s preferred). Experience: Minimum 3 years of experience in simulation operations, healthcare education technology, or a related technical field requiring independent judgment. Experience working within an academic institution and/or in healthcare simulation preferred. Demonstrated expertise in high-fidelity simulation technology, AV systems, and instructional technology integration. Skills & Abilities: Advanced troubleshooting and problem-solving skills. Strong project management, organizational, and interpersonal communication skills. Ability to work independently, make decisions, and prioritize in a fast-paced environment. Commitment to supporting collaborative, inclusive, and high-quality educational experiences. Ability to safely lift 35 pounds. Commitment to working collegially within the College and University.  Demonstrate an attitude and behavior that reflects the core values and mission of Gwynedd Mercy University. Application:   To apply for the position, please complete and submit an application through the Gwynedd Mercy University careers webpage: www.gmercyu.edu/careers The following documents are required: Cover letter & resume List of three professional references Transcripts Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. The anticipated start date is fall 2026. For questions about this position, please contact: Dr. Kristin Sagedy at: sagedy.k@gmercyu.edu Gwynedd Mercy University:   Gwynedd Mercy University is a Catholic University, rooted in the Mercy tradition of service to society. Gwynedd Mercy University believes that diversity in our campus community enriches the educational experience of its students by providing them with the opportunity to learn from individuals of various backgrounds. The University encourages individuals from traditionally underrepresented groups to apply.  Gwynedd Mercy University is committed to maintaining a positive learning, working, and living environment that is free from unlawful discrimination and harassment. Gwynedd Mercy University does not discriminate against any applicant for admission to or employment at the University because of race, religion, age, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, color, marital status, veteran status, genetic characteristics, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law (Protected Classes). This includes, but is not limited to, all terms and conditions of employment including, but not limited to, recruitment, selection, hiring, placement, transfer, promotion, training, compensation, benefits, discipline, and termination. The University will not tolerate unlawful acts of discrimination or harassment based upon Protected Classes, or related retaliation against or by any employee or student.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=26d7eba1-7ff1-4642-a01e-31acc49de6f5&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201786361639_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Media Support Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-02\n\nLocation: Gwynedd Valley\n\nPosition Summary We are looking for someone who knows audiovisual technology, enjoys solving problems, and takes pride in making things work well. Just as important, this person needs to work effectively with a wide variety of people, including faculty, staff, students, senior leaders, vendors, guests, and other members of the IT team. The right person will be technically curious, dependable, calm under pressure, and willing to take ownership of a problem through resolution. The Media Support Specialist is the University's primary audiovisual and classroom technology specialist. This person takes ownership of the technology in our classrooms, meeting spaces, presentation spaces, and other University venues and helps make sure that it is reliable, well maintained, and ready when people need it. Essential Responsibilities Take primary ownership of the University's audiovisual, classroom, meeting, and presentation technology environments. Regularly evaluate the condition and reliability of AV systems and identify problems before they interfere with classes, meetings, or events. Troubleshoot and resolve AV and related technology problems and coordinate repairs or vendor support when needed. Provide technical support for University events and serve as the lead AV resource for complex or high-profile events. Work with other trained members of the User Services team so that routine event support can be shared across the department. Evaluate AV technologies and make recommendations for new equipment, upgrades, replacements, standards, and improvements. Work with vendors on installations, repairs, upgrades, and projects and help ensure that solutions meet University needs. Develop documentation and train other members of the User Services team so that AV knowledge and support are not dependent on one person. Work closely with faculty, staff, students, event coordinators, Plant Services, vendors, and other University departments to understand needs, resolve problems, and coordinate technology support. Participate as a member of the broader User Services and Institutional Technology Services teams and assist with other technical support responsibilities when needed. Provide occasional evening and weekend support for University events. Qualifications We are more interested in what you can do, how you approach problems, and how you work with people than in whether you meet every item on a checklist. The successful candidate will have: Hands-on experience supporting audiovisual, classroom, conferencing, presentation, or related technologies. Enough knowledge of AV technology to troubleshoot problems, evaluate solutions, work effectively with vendors, and make informed recommendations. Strong troubleshooting and problem-solving skills, including the willingness to investigate unfamiliar problems and figure out how to resolve them. A strong sense of ownership. If something is not working properly, you take responsibility for getting it addressed rather than waiting for someone else to solve it. The ability to remain calm, professional, and resourceful when technology fails at an inconvenient time. Strong communication and interpersonal skills and the ability to work effectively with people who have different priorities, responsibilities, personalities, and levels of technical knowledge. The judgment and confidence to listen, explain options, negotiate competing needs, and recommend practical solutions. A collaborative approach and a willingness to share knowledge and help other members of the team develop their AV skills. Strong organizational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities with limited supervision. A willingness to continue learning as technologies and University needs change. Relevant education, technical training, certifications, or professional experience may all provide the background necessary to succeed in this position. A bachelor's degree is not required. Experience with higher education technology, digital signage, video conferencing, streaming, AV control systems, event production, or similar technologies is helpful but not required. Familiarity with the specific products used at Gwynedd Mercy University is also helpful, but we would rather hire someone with strong technical fundamentals, good judgment, curiosity, and the ability to learn. Physical Requirements This is a hands-on position that requires the ability to move throughout campus classrooms and event spaces, work with installed technology and equipment, and occasionally lift or move equipment weighing up to 50 pounds. Reasonable accommodations may be made to enable individuals with disabilities to perform the essential functions of the position. General Statement This description is intended to describe the general nature and level of work performed in this position. It is not an exhaustive list of every responsibility or qualification. Duties may change as technology and University needs evolve. EOE Statement: Gwynedd Mercy University is committed to maintaining a positive learning, working, and living environment that is free from unlawful discrimination and harassment. Gwynedd Mercy University does not discriminate against any applicant for admission to or employment at the University because of race, religion, age, gender, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability, color, marital status, veteran status, genetic characteristics, or any other characteristic protected by federal, state, or local law (‘Protected Classes’). This includes, but is not limited to, admissions, financial aid, educational services, and student programs and activities, as well as to all terms and conditions of employment including, but not limited to, recruitment, selection, hiring, placement, transfer, promotion, training, compensation, benefits, discipline, and termination. The University will not tolerate unlawful acts of discrimination or harassment based upon Protected Classes, or related retaliation against or by any employee or student.",
      "department": ""
    },
